    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the RAAS pathway. Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril? Specifically, how do they interact with the angiotensin conversion process to lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, blocks the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), which is responsible for converting angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor and also stimulates aldosterone secretion, both of which lead to increased blood pressure. By inhibiting the formation of angiotensin II, lisinopril reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone levels, leading to vasodilation and decreased blood volume as a result of reduced aldosterone-induced sodium and water reabsorption. These actions work together to lower blood pressure in patients with hypertension.",
      "source": "target",
