    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the enzyme-substrate interaction side of things? Like, how does the inhibitor actually physically interact with the ACE enzyme to prevent that conversion from happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) typically has a shallow active site with a defined structure that fits both angiotensin I and the necessary cofactor to facilitate the necessary enzymatic reaction to produce angiotensin II. The ACE inhibitors, such as lisinopril, are designed to mimic the structure of angiotensin I very closely, fitting into the active site of the enzyme in a manner that is similar to that of the natural substrate. The inhibitor forms a temporary and stable complex with the active site of ACE – that resembles the transition state of the substrate but does not undergo the same chemical changes throughout the reaction. This form of an \"inactive\" complex means that the actual conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II is blocked due to the unwieldy positioning of the inhibitor, which prevents the necessary chemical steps from completing. Lisinopril, in particular, mimics the part of angiotensin I that occupies a key position in binding the cofactor and facilitating conversion through its essential side chain, leading to the inhibition of the normal physiological change. Therefore, Lisinopril effectively \"traps\" the ACE enzyme, leading to a decreased synthesis of angiotensin II until endogenous processes can overcome the inhibitory",
      "source": "target",
